# 🚨 REGRESSION FIXTURE — THE TRAP ROW. B2: the `&`-projection with a BARE-PARAM
# root. Asserts 11 / 10.
#
# `via_bare` takes `Holder h` BARE, which under CoW-default-borrow is a BORROW.
# The write `bump_int(&h.i)` therefore MATERIALIZES a private copy at the
# closest immutable context: the callee observes its own write, and the CALLER'S
# object is UNCHANGED. ggdef agrees: 11 / 10.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS THE ROW MOST LIKELY TO REGRESS SILENTLY. A fix that makes the
# `&`-projection write THROUGH to the root looks correct on every other fixture
# in the batch and is WRONG here — it would print 11 / 11, turning a borrow into
# a mutable alias and breaking CoW's central guarantee. Landing 11 / 10 (not
# merely keeping the caller's 10, which the fully-broken compiler also did) is
# what separates a correct fix from a plausible one.
#
# AXIS + CELL (Core #12): the ROOT-SHAPE axis of the `&`-argument face, at the
# bare-parameter cell — the one root shape that must NOT write through. The
# owned-local cell is `sound_amp_byvalue_place_writethrough.gg`.
#
# RED-VERIFIED: against the pre-fix compiler this printed 10 / 10 — the caller's
# 10 right by accident, the write lost inside the callee too.
#
# GUARDED MECHANISM: the ORDER of the Family-1 chokepoint. `try_resolve_place`
# is called AFTER the G2 root-materialize in `lower_call_arg`, so it resolves a
# place in the private copy `cow_before_mutation` rebound the name to. Hoist that
# call above the G2 block and this fixture prints 11 / 11 while every other
# fixture in the batch stays green.
#
# The seed is computed, not a literal.
#
# Cited from TODO.md (CoW / ownership / High): "By-value-field `&`-arg
# write-through is SILENTLY LOST" (bare-param-root row).
struct Holder:
    int i

int seed():
    return 10

void bump_int(int &n):
    n = n + 1

void via_bare(Holder h):
    bump_int(&h.i)
    print(h.i)

void main():
    Holder h = Holder(seed())
    via_bare(h)
    print(h.i)
